course: biology
lesson: 89
title: The amphibian room: poison frogs, axolotls and the frog rescue
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In the amphibian room tiny frogs as bright as sweets hop about; in the wild a poison dart frog's colours shout «I taste horrible», and the golden poison frog of Colombia is one of the most poisonous animals on Earth.
explain: Zoo-bred dart frogs are harmless, because their poison comes from the ants and mites they eat in the wild, yet the keeper still wears gloves, this time to protect the frog's thin, damp skin, which it partly breathes through.
explain: A pink axolotl smiles from its tank with feathery gills like a baby that never grew up, and a sign warns that a fungus is killing frogs all over the world, so zoos keep rescue groups safe like a Noah's Ark.
explain: In the fairy tale a kiss turns a frog into a prince, but in real life kissing a frog or toad turns nothing into anything and some skins hurt your mouth, and no, holding a toad does not give you warts.
fact: Wild poison dart frogs have poison in their skin, and their bright colours warn predators; zoo-bred ones are not poisonous because their poison comes from the ants and mites they eat in the wild.
fact: The golden poison frog of Colombia is one of the most poisonous animals in the world, and some people in Colombia wiped darts on frogs' backs for hunting, which gave dart frogs their name.
fact: Frogs breathe and take in water partly through their thin, moist skin, so tanks are kept damp, and gloves protect their skin from the oils and soap on our hands.
fact: Chytrid fungus attacks frog skin and has killed frogs in many countries, wiping out some kinds; it spread partly when people moved frogs around the world.
fact: Zoos keep rescue groups of rare frogs in clean rooms until the wild is safe again.
fact: Axolotls are salamanders that keep their feathery gills and live in water all their lives; they come from lakes near Mexico City and are almost gone in the wild, though millions live in labs and homes.
fact: Axolotls can regrow lost legs and even parts of the heart and brain; they have three feathery gill stalks on each side of the head.
fact: The olm lives in dark caves in Slovenia and nearby; it has no colour, and its eyes are hidden under skin, because in total darkness eyes were no use and faded over many generations; it can live about 100 years.
fact: The Chinese giant salamander is the largest amphibian in the world, up to about 1.8 metres long, and is critically endangered from hunting and changed rivers.
fact: Male frogs fill a vocal sac with air to make their calls louder, telling females where they are and what kind of frog they are.
fact: Kissing a frog will not make a prince, and many toads have poison glands behind their eyes; dogs that bite cane toads in Australia can get very ill.
fact: Warts on people are caused by a virus that passes between people, not by toads; the bumps on a toad are glands.
fact: Glass frogs have see-through bellies, and in 2022 scientists found that a sleeping glass frog hides most of its red blood in its liver to look almost clear.
exercise: Why are wild poison dart frogs so brightly coloured? | To warn predators off | To attract the visitors who come to the rainforest | To hide among bright flowers | To keep warm in the sun
exercise: Why are zoo-bred poison dart frogs not poisonous? | Their poison comes from wild ants and mites they do not get in zoos | Keepers carefully remove the poison from their skin every single week | Glass tanks soak up the poison | They are a different, harmless kind
exercise: Which is one of the most poisonous animals in the world? | The golden poison frog | The axolotl | The olm | The common frog
exercise: How did dart frogs get their name? | Some people in Colombia wiped hunting darts on their backs | They dart about the forest floor faster than any other frog | They are shaped like darts | They were found on a dartboard
exercise: Why does the keeper spray the frog tank with water? | Frogs breathe partly through moist skin | To wash the poison off the frogs' backs | To make the frogs croak | To clean the glass for visitors
exercise: Why does the keeper wear gloves with zoo frogs? | To protect the frog's skin | Because zoo frogs are just as deadly as wild ones | To keep her hands warm in the cold room | Because frogs bite very hard
exercise: What is killing frogs all over the world? | Chytrid fungus | A shortage of flies to eat in the summer | Too many princes kissing them | Loud music in cities
exercise: How did chytrid fungus spread around the world? | Partly by people moving frogs from place to place | By flying in on the feathers of birds and nothing else | It came from outer space | It grew from rubbish in the sea
exercise: What is special about an axolotl? | It keeps its baby gills for life | It turns into a frog after a year and hops out of the lake | It can glide short distances between trees | It lives in dry deserts, far from water
exercise: Where do wild axolotls come from? | Lakes near Mexico City | Caves in Slovenia | Rivers in China | Forests in Colombia
exercise: Why does the olm have no eyes you can see? | In darkness eyes were not needed and faded over generations | It was born without a head | It hides its eyes from the light by shutting them tight all its life | Keepers covered them with tape
exercise: Which is the largest amphibian in the world? | The Chinese giant salamander | The axolotl | The cane toad | The golden poison frog
exercise: Why does a frog's throat swell like a balloon? | It is calling, using its vocal sac | It is swallowing a big insect whole | It is blowing itself up to look big and scary to snakes | It is holding its breath underwater
exercise: Does touching a toad give you warts? | No, human warts come from a virus passed between people | Yes, every time you touch one, a wart grows on your hand | Only if the toad is wet | Only on the hand that touches it
exercise: What happens if you kiss a frog? | Nothing magic, and some skins can hurt your mouth | It turns into a prince, just as it does in the fairy tale | It turns into a princess | It grows bigger
exercise: How does a sleeping glass frog become almost see-through? | It hides most of its red blood in its liver | It stops its heart for the whole night so no blood shows | It sheds its skin | It drinks clear water until it is clear
test: In the fairy tale a princess kisses a frog and gets a prince. «worth a try?» | No; it does nothing magic, and some frogs have skin poisons | Yes, but only green frogs | Yes, if you kiss it at midnight under a full moon, it really works | Only toads turn into princes
test: «my nan says touch a toad and you get warts» What is right? | Warts come from a human virus; toad bumps are glands | Your nan is right, toads spread warts to anyone who picks them up | Only frogs give warts, not toads | Warts come from eating frogs
test: An axolotl has 4 legs, 1 tail and 3 gill stalks on each side. How many gill stalks altogether? | 6 | 3 | 4 | 8
test: 5 frogs in the tank, sorry 6, one was behind the leaf. how many legs | 24 | 20 | 30 | 12
test: What would happen if a frog's skin dried out? | It could not breathe properly | It would just breathe faster through its nose instead | It would slowly turn into a toad | Nothing, frog skin is waterproof
test: «axolotls are everywhere in pet shops, how can they be endangered» What is right? | Wild ones are almost gone, even though many live in homes | They are not endangered at all, there are millions of them in the lakes | Pet ones are a different animal | Only pink ones are endangered
test: Your mate wants to pick up a bright yellow frog on holiday in Colombia. What should you say? | Do not touch it; wild bright frogs can be deadly poisonous | Fine, all frogs are harmless | Fine, as long as you wash your hands well with soap afterwards | Only red frogs are poisonous
test: Why would a fungus that attacks skin be so deadly to frogs? | Frogs need their skin to breathe and take in water | Frogs eat the fungus by mistake and choke on it | The fungus makes them too slow to jump | Frog skin is their only food
test: Why would animals living in dark caves lose their eyes over many generations? | In total darkness eyes are no use, so they faded away | They poke their eyes out on the sharp rocks in the dark | The cold freezes their eyes | Bats steal their eyes
test: «why's that frog's throat blowing up like bubblegum» What is right? | A male is filling his vocal sac to call louder | It swallowed a big gulp of air by mistake and can't burp | It is about to pop | It is sick and needs a vet
test: Why might a see-through frog be hard for a bird to spot on a leaf? | Light passes through, so it looks like part of the leaf | It hides under the leaf all day and never comes out to feed | Birds cannot see the colour green | It is too small to eat
test: A dog in Australia bites a cane toad in the garden. What is likely? | The dog can get very ill from the toad's poison | The dog gets warts all over its mouth and nose | The toad turns into a prince | Nothing, toads are harmless
exam: Identify the principal reason for which poison dart frogs bred in zoological collections are not toxic. | Their toxins derive from wild ants and mites absent in captivity | Their toxins are surgically removed by keepers at regular intervals | Glass enclosures neutralise all toxins | They belong to an entirely different family
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the chytrid fungus. | It attacks frog skin and was spread partly by people moving frogs | It is beneficial to frogs and improves their breathing through the skin | It affects only frogs maintained in zoos | It is transmitted to frogs by human warts
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the axolotl? | A salamander which keeps its juvenile gills and remains aquatic | A frog which loses its gills within a year and thereafter lives on land | A fish native to the caves of Slovenia | A lizard capable of flight
exam: Ascertain the cause of warts upon the human skin. | A virus transmitted between persons | Contact with the skin of toads | The consumption of frogs | Exposure to the chytrid fungus
